# Reflective formal system

A **reflective formal system** is a formal theory that carries an effective
encoding of its own syntax and represents its own proof relations internally.
The term, and the narrower **Gödelian strange loop** defined from it, were
proposed in the discussion behind this vault. Neither is established
mathematical vocabulary. They are the result of an attempt to replace
Hofstadter's informal notion by a definition "as formal as a set theory
textbook", and they define a class by its machinery and a subclass by a
theorem.

## Description

### The thesis and the move

The starting position was that the only precise and mathematical strange loop
is Gödel numbering with the incompleteness proof, and that a definition
should reproduce Gödel's steps. Five properties were proposed: a
self-referencing, formally well-defined system; completeness and consistency;
definition by example; irreducibility; and a list of anti-examples.

The reply did not turn those five into one definition. It observed that
"strange loop" is not a standard mathematical class and that forcing all the
examples under one formal heading would conflate them. It defined two
things in sequence.

### Step one: the class

A reflective formal system is described schematically by a tuple

$$(\mathcal L,\ T,\ \gamma,\ \mathcal R).$$

| Component | What it is | In Peano arithmetic |
|---|---|---|
| $\mathcal L$ | a formal language | the language of arithmetic |
| $T$ | a theory in that language, with a computable set of axioms | the Peano axioms |
| $\gamma$ | an effective encoding of the syntax of $\mathcal L$ by objects $T$ talks about | a [[godel-numbering]] |
| $\mathcal R$ | formulas of $\mathcal L$ that represent syntactic and proof relations on the codes | $\operatorname{Proof}_T(p,x)$ and $\operatorname{Prov}_T(x)$ |

The first two components are any formal system. The last two are what make
it reflective: the system has names for its own expressions, and it can
state, in its own language, which of them are proofs of which.

This is *static* reflection. The system expresses properties of its own
syntax. Nothing changes over time, and nothing is fed back.

### Step two: the subclass

A **Gödelian strange loop** is a reflective formal system that additionally
has a diagonal [[fixed-point]] producing a *provable limitation* of the
system, such as incompleteness under precise hypotheses. For arithmetic that
fixed point is the [[godel-sentence]], and the limitation is the first of the
[[incompleteness-theorems]].

The two steps correspond to the two distinctions drawn later in the
discussion. Step one is the existence of self-reference. Step two is a
theorem about its consequences.

### What happened to the five properties

- **Self-referencing and formally well defined:** kept, and made exact as the
  components $\gamma$ and $\mathcal R$.
- **Complete and consistent:** corrected. Consistency is a hypothesis.
  Completeness is what the theorem excludes for such systems.
- **By example, including human intelligence:** the Gödel case is an instance.
  Human intelligence is a conjectural analogy and satisfies no clause of the
  definition.
- **Irreducible:** replaced by "provable limitation", after the word was found
  to cover three unrelated notions. See [[undecidability]].
- **Anti-examples:** confirmed. Factorial, fractals and ordinary cyclic
  feedback have neither $\gamma$ nor $\mathcal R$.

### What the definition leaves out

It was written for one example and fits that example.

- Escher's hands and Bach's canon have no language, theory or encoding. They
  remain strange loops in Hofstadter's sense and are outside this definition.
- The [[constructible-universe]] describes itself through definability and
  absoluteness, with no diagonal sentence. The discussion calls it a second
  rigorous example of a different form.
- A theory revised over time by a person or an agent is not one system of
  this kind but a sequence of them. See [[reflection-progression]].

Among the seven candidate definitions collected at the end of the discussion,
this card supplies the third, "reflective", and the fourth, "Gödelian".

### Running it and proving it are different things

The discussion sketches a formal experiment in HQL: implement syntax trees,
an effective encoding, substitution, proof checking and the diagonal
construction. It attaches one warning. Executing the construction in a
programming language shows that the encoding and the diagonal sentence can be
computed. It does not show that the theory *represents* those computations
internally, which is what the component $\mathcal R$ asserts and what the
theorems use. The first is a program run from outside. The second is a proof
about the theory. A vault that executes a description of itself is in the
same position: execution from outside is not yet internal representation.

## Relations

- It requires a [[godel-numbering]] {relation: uses} as its encoding component.
- Its Gödelian subclass is defined by a [[fixed-point]] {relation: uses} with a provable consequence.
- That fixed point is, for arithmetic, the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: example-of}.
- The provable limitation it asks for is given by the [[incompleteness-theorems]] {relation: uses}.
- Its representation component includes the [[provability-predicate]] {relation: part-of}.
- It is one proposed sharpening of [[strange-loop]] {relation: is-a}, narrower than Hofstadter's notion.
- It supplies two entries of [[definitions-of-strange-loop]] {relation: part-of}.
- It is the static case, against the evolving case of a [[reflection-progression]] {relation: contrasts}.
- It does not cover the [[constructible-universe]] {relation: distinguished-from}, whose self-description has another mechanism.
- It is a formal instance of the split between [[object-and-meta-level]] {relation: uses}: the encoding brings the meta level inside.
- It bears on the question [[strange-loop-meaning]] {relation: answers}: it is the most formal answer the vault has, and it covers one case.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[godel-sentence]].** A sentence provably equivalent to its own
  unprovability. It is the object that promotes a reflective system to a
  Gödelian one.
- **[[godel-numbering]].** The assignment of numbers to formulas and proofs.
  It is the component of the tuple without which the others have nothing to
  speak about.
- **[[reflection-progression]].** A sequence of theories, each extending the
  last. It is what the discussion turned to when it noticed that this
  definition contains no time and no revision.
- **[[definitions-of-strange-loop]].** Seven candidates from Hofstadter's to
  an epistemically bounded one. This card is where the two formal ones are
  worked out.

